WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.780
Stay up all night.

2
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.680
Near dawn, you can barely keep your eyes open.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.780
By early afternoon, still without sleep, you feel better.

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.600
How can more time awake make you less sleepy?

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.260
If time awake were all that mattered, the afternoon would feel worse.

6
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.680
Something else changed: a clock.

7
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:29.653
The first process is sleep pressure.

8
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:33.753
It builds while you are awake, quickly at first, then more slowly.

9
00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:35.713
While you sleep, it drains.

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:39.433
The second process is a clock in your brain.

11
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:42.993
It keeps a cycle of about a day, even with no windows.

12
00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:46.123
The clock's main push is toward waking.

13
00:00:47.043 --> 00:00:51.023
It is hardest in the late afternoon and evening, weakest in the early morning.

14
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:53.433
Put both on one graph.

15
00:00:54.293 --> 00:00:58.553
An upper wavy line is the level pressure must reach before you fall asleep.

16
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:03.503
A lower wavy line is the level it must drain to before you wake.

17
00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:06.203
Both lines rise and fall with the clock.

18
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:11.893
Here is the one idea, in the simple model of an adult's undisturbed sleep.

19
00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:16.053
Sleep pressure climbs while you are awake and drains while you sleep.

20
00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:19.666
You fall asleep when it climbs to an upper level.

21
00:01:20.486 --> 00:01:22.266
You wake when it drains to a lower one.

22
00:01:23.046 --> 00:01:26.406
The clock raises both levels by day and lowers them by night.

23
00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:33.410
Why two levels? Replace them with one line midway between, and the model chatters on

24
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:34.390
and off at that line.

25
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.670
The gap keeps this model's sleep in one long block.

26
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:39.873
Back to the all-nighter.

27
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:44.693
Staying up means refusing sleep after pressure crosses the upper level.

28
00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:47.773
Near dawn, the clock has pulled that level low.

29
00:01:48.613 --> 00:01:51.353
So pressure sits far above it, and you feel very sleepy.

30
00:01:52.466 --> 00:01:55.026
By early afternoon, pressure is higher still.

31
00:01:55.846 --> 00:01:58.686
But the clock has lifted the level, so the excess has shrunk.

32
00:01:59.716 --> 00:02:02.956
When you finally sleep, will you get back every hour you lost?

33
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.300
No. Recovery sleep is longer than usual, but nowhere near hour for hour.

34
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.220
Much of the payback comes as deeper sleep.

35
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.340
Can the two processes be pulled apart?

36
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.140
In rats, destroy the clock: the suprachiasmatic nucleus, just above the optic

37
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.140
chiasm.

38
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.610
Sleep scatters across day and night.

39
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.510
Yet after a spell without sleep, these rats still slept more, and more deeply.

40
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.120
Keep this. Pressure climbs and drains, and the clock sets the levels.
